  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p>At 2,021 feet, <strong>Baker Mountain</strong> is a summit in Arkansas. The Baker Mountain Trail covers 11.1 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 486 feet of elevation gain \u2014 gentle grades, but the mileage is what makes it a Hard outing.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>Most of the climbing comes early \u2014 the first half of the route does the bulk of the work before the grade eases. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 0.79, gaining roughly 400 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 1,129 feet, the route tops out near 1,499 feet.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=34.48837,-94.13606\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">34.48837, -94.13606</a> (tap for driving directions). Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>This trail is hikeable most of the year. Summer brings heat and humidity \u2014 carry extra water \u2014 while fall and spring offer the most comfortable conditions. Ice is possible on shaded stretches in mid-winter.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Hard rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 4.0 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Boar Tusk Mountain, Bee Mountain and Eagle Mountain \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
